data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1: Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?** The ask is open in Asks with two buttons, Redis (a1) and Postgres (a2). Click one there, or tell me here and I'll resolve it.\n\n**The short version:** carts have grown past what fits in the 4 KB session cookie, so they need a home on the server. The question is which storage to use.\n\n**Example:** a shopper adds 30 items with names, prices and quantities. That's more than the cookie can hold, so the browser would drop it. The fix is to keep the cart on the server and store only a small cart ID in the cookie.\n\n**Option a1, Redis:**\n- Very fast, and built for short-lived data like sessions and carts.\n- Carts can expire automatically after a set time.\n- If Redis restarts without persistence turned on, carts are lost.\n- It's an extra system to run, unless the app already uses it.\n- Cart data is hard to query for things like \"abandoned carts from last week.\"\n\n**Option a2, Postgres (the main agent's pick):**\n- Carts survive restarts, and the database is already the source of truth for products and orders.\n- Carts can be queried and joined with products and orders, which makes abandoned-cart reports and emails straightforward.\n- Slightly slower per request than Redis, but fine at normal cart traffic.\n- Old carts need a scheduled cleanup job, since nothing expires them automatically.\n\n**My take:** Postgres, unless the team already runs Redis for other things or cart traffic is very high. Losing carts on a restart is the worst outcome here, and Postgres avoids that with one less system to operate.\n\nI haven't checked the checkout to confirm what the app already runs. The recommendation rests on the ask's description."}}

